@PACK Fishing Guide
@ENTRY basics|How to Fish: The Basics
Fishing is simpler than it looks: present something a fish wants to eat, in a place fish are holding, and be ready when one bites. Start with a basic spinning rod and reel, some hooks, split-shot weights, a bobber, and either live bait or a few soft plastic lures.

Rig a simple setup: tie a hook to your line with a clinch knot (pass the line through the eye, wrap it 5 or 6 times, back through the loop, and cinch), pinch a small weight a foot above the hook, and clip a bobber higher up. Bait the hook with a worm, cast out, and watch the bobber. When it dips under, gently sweep the rod up to set the hook, then reel steadily.

Fish hold near structure: weed edges, submerged logs, drop-offs, dock pilings, and current seams. Early morning and evening are prime. Move if nothing bites in 20 minutes; you go to the fish, they don't come to you.
@END
@ENTRY gear|Bait, Lures, and Knots
Live bait is the great equalizer, worms, minnows, crickets, and grasshoppers catch nearly everything. Hook a worm so it wiggles; hook a minnow through the lips or back so it swims. Keep bait cool and lively.

Lures let you cover water fast. A small inline spinner or a soft plastic grub on a jighead catches bass, trout, and panfish across the country. Cast past your target, let it sink, and retrieve with occasional pauses; most strikes come on the fall or the pause.

Learn two knots and you're set: the improved clinch (above) for tying on hooks and lures, and the loop knot or a simple palomar for strength. Wet your knots before cinching so friction doesn't weaken the line, and trim the tag end close.
@END
@ENTRY handling|Landing and Handling Fish
Bring a fish in steadily without horsing it; let the rod's flex tire it out. Wet your hands before touching a fish, dry hands strip the protective slime coat and can harm fish you release.

If you're keeping the fish, dispatch it quickly and humanely with a firm knock to the head, then get it on ice. A fish left flopping in a warm bucket spoils fast and tastes muddy. Keep only what you'll eat and know the local limits and license rules.

To release a fish, back the hook out gently, support it upright in the water, and let it swim off on its own. If it's hooked deep, cut the line close rather than digging around; many hooks dissolve or work free.
@END
@ENTRY clean|How to Clean a Fish
Cleaning a fish is quick once you've done a couple. You'll need a sharp knife and a clean surface. There are two goals: remove the guts, and remove either the scales (to cook whole) or the fillets (for boneless pieces).

To gut and scale for whole cooking: scrape scales off tail-to-head with the back of a knife or a spoon, rinsing as you go. Then slide the knife into the belly at the vent and cut forward to the gills, open the belly, and pull out all the guts. Remove the dark bloodline along the spine with a thumbnail. Rinse the cavity clean.

To fillet for boneless meat: cut down behind the gill to the backbone, turn the blade flat, and slice along the spine toward the tail, riding the bones. Flip and repeat on the other side. Lay each fillet skin-down and slide the knife between meat and skin to remove it. Rinse, check for stray bones, and you have clean fillets.
@END
@ENTRY cookfry|Cooking Fish: Pan-Fried
Pan-frying is the classic shore lunch and hard to beat. Pat the fillets or cleaned whole fish dry, season with salt and pepper, and dredge in flour or cornmeal (seasoned cornmeal gives a crisp, golden crust).

Heat oil or butter in a skillet over medium coals until it shimmers. Lay the fish in away from you and don't crowd the pan. Cook 3 to 4 minutes per side depending on thickness, flipping once, until the crust is golden and the flesh is opaque and flakes easily with a fork.

Fish cooks fast and turns dry if overdone, pull it the moment it flakes. A squeeze of lemon and a pinch of salt at the end is all it needs. Fillets fry quicker than whole fish; thick trout may need a few extra minutes with a lid.
@END
@ENTRY cookother|Cooking Fish: Foil, Grill, and Coals
Beyond the frying pan, fish loves a few other camp methods. In foil: lay a cleaned fish or fillet on buttered heavy foil, top with lemon slices, a pat of butter, salt, pepper, and herbs, seal into a packet, and set on medium coals 10 to 15 minutes until it flakes.

On a grate: oil the grate well (fish loves to stick), and grill firm fish like trout, salmon, or bass over medium coals a few minutes per side. Whole fish grill beautifully; slash the skin and stuff the cavity with lemon and herbs. Handle gently when turning.

Straight on the coals or a hot rock: a whole fish wrapped in wet leaves or foil bakes in its own steam buried near the embers. However you cook it, the doneness cue is the same, opaque flesh that flakes cleanly off the bone. Undercooked fish is translucent and rubbery; give it another minute.
@END
@ENTRY safety|Water Safety and Ethics
A day of fishing is only good if everyone comes home. Wear a life jacket in any boat and near fast or deep water; most fishing drownings involve no life vest. Watch footing on slick rocks and steep banks, and never wade water you can't read.

Respect the resource: buy your license, follow size and bag limits, and don't take more than you'll use. These rules keep fisheries healthy for the next person and the next generation. Pack out every scrap of line and tackle, discarded line kills birds and wildlife for years.

Clean fish away from camp and dispose of guts properly, downstream and away from where people swim or draw water, or pack them out. Wash hands and surfaces well; raw fish and its juices can carry bacteria. Leave the spot better than you found it.
@END
